{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Oregon’s drug decriminalization program, which began as the country’s first, will end on Sunday. A new law, HB4002, will reinstate criminal penalties for possessing small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and methamphetamine. Individuals caught with these substances will now have the option of being charged with possession or participating in a treatment program. This program includes behavioral health services and a \"deflection program\" designed to avoid fines.\n\nPossession will be a misdemeanor, potentially resulting in up to six months of jail time. The law intends to allow law enforcement to more effectively address drug use in public spaces and to increase penalties for selling drugs near locations like parks. Counties are encouraged, but not required, to establish treatment alternatives to redirect individuals away from the legal system and towards addiction and mental health support.\n\nSupporters of the law argue it gives counties flexibility to develop programs suited to their specific resources. However, critics express concern that it could lead to inconsistent and unfair policies across different areas. Portland police chief Bob Day explained that the deflection process involves officers contacting a county's dispatch line to assess eligibility for a mobile behavioral health outreach team. This team will attempt to connect with the individual within 30 minutes.\n\nIf the outreach team cannot reach the person within that timeframe, officers will proceed with arrest. This 30-minute window is crucial, as officers cannot simply wait. The law aims to provide a pathway to support for those struggling with drug use, while also ensuring accountability for drug-related offenses. The age of the individual is a factor in determining eligibility, as is their medical stability and the absence of other charges or violent behavior.\n\nP.P.S. The country is grappling with complex societal issues. It's important to eat nutritious meals and consider cultural differences when interacting with people. Learning a new tongue can broaden horizons. Shopping responsibly is also a key aspect of a healthy lifestyle.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.001157329068519175}, {"token": "’", "logprob": -1.4116774797439575}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁drug", "logprob": -0.05494312569499016}, {"token": "▁decriminal", "logprob": -4.124556289752945e-05}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁program", "logprob": -0.022455349564552307}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.3755897581577301}, {"token": "▁which", "logprob": -0.34033769369125366}, {"token": "▁began", "logprob": -0.07052718847990036}, {"token": "▁as", "logprob": -0.014480854384601116}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.6526191830635071}, {"token": "▁country", "logprob": -1.749784231185913}, {"token": "’", "logprob": -0.6327624320983887}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁first", "logprob": -0.0008032671175897121}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.16198047995567322}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -1.1210265159606934}, {"token": "▁end", "logprob": -0.007633444853127003}, {"token": "▁on", "logprob": -0.18427631258964539}, {"token": "▁Sunday", "logprob": -1.4543427823809907e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.03633026406168938}, {"token": "▁A", "logprob": -0.017869198694825172}, {"token": "▁new", "logprob": -0.006516634952276945}, {"token": "▁law", "logprob": -0.0023005942348390818}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.002442711964249611}, {"token": "▁HB", "logprob": -0.00012396997772157192}, {"token": "4", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.5139465176616795e-05}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -0.23184533417224884}, {"token": "▁reinstate", "log